course: arts
lesson: 8
title: Singing, choirs and talent shows
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your voice is an instrument you carry everywhere: breath from the lungs makes the vocal folds in your throat shake, and steady breath, not shouting, is what makes a voice strong and in tune.
explain: Singers warm up with humming and lip trills (blowing "brrrr" through loose lips) so the voice works without strain; it looks like a stable of horses, but professionals do it before every show.
explain: A choir often splits into parts singing different notes at once, called harmony, so a different note is not always a mistake; picture the high voices, the low voices and the middle voices fitting together like layers of a cake.
explain: Most people who think they are "tone deaf" just have not practised; real tone deafness is rare, nerves make even good voices shake, and a sore throat needs rest, not a big push.
explain: Scots say "cannae" for can't, "lassies" for girls, "dinnae greet" for don't cry, "braw" for great and "hen" for dear; in Northern Ireland a "header" is a wild or mad person, and in Wales "lush" means lovely.
fact: Nerves make breath short and the voice shaky, even in good singers; slow breathing before singing helps calm the voice.
fact: Many boys sing very high before their voices change; cathedral choirs have high voices called trebles, and boys' voices usually drop in the teenage years.
fact: Warm-ups such as lip trills and humming help the breath and the voice muscles work without strain.
fact: TV talent shows are edited to be dramatic, so harsh comments and tears are often shown more because they get more viewers.
fact: The diaphragm is a big muscle under the lungs that helps you breathe in; "singing from the diaphragm" means steady breath support, not shouting.
fact: True tone deafness, called amusia, is reported to be rare; most people can learn to sing in tune much better with practice.
fact: Harmony means singing different notes at the same time that sound good together.
fact: Singing styles differ around the world: Chinese folk and opera singing, Ukrainian folk singing and Western pop use the voice in different ways.
fact: Lip-syncing means moving your lips to a recording instead of singing live; there have been reported cases of famous singers lip-syncing at big events, and people argue whether it is cheating or a sensible way to protect a big show from going wrong.
fact: Singing with a sore throat can strain the voice and make it worse; singers rest, drink water and avoid shouting.
fact: Feelings after a performance can be big and mixed, and feeling bad does not prove you sang badly.
fact: Vibrato is a controlled wobble in the voice that singers use on purpose, different from a nervous shake.
exercise: Iryna's voice shook on her first audition note. What does it mean? | Nerves often shake even good voices, so she can keep going | She cannot really sing and should give up auditions for good | Her voice is broken for good | Only bad singers' voices ever shake
exercise: Harry says boys can't sing high. What is true? | Young boys often have very high voices, called trebles | Boys' voices are low from the day they are born, so they can't | Only girls are allowed in cathedral choirs | High notes damage boys' throats for ever
exercise: When do boys' voices usually drop? | In the teenage years | Around the age of three | Only after the age of forty | They never change at all
exercise: Jack laughs at the "brrrr" lip trills. Why do singers do them? | They warm the voice up gently and help stop strain | To make the audience laugh a little before the show starts | They are only for horses, not people | They make the voice permanently lower
exercise: Maya cries at the harsh judge on TV. What should she remember? | Talent shows are edited to be dramatic | TV always shows exactly what happened, in the right order | Judges are paid extra to make children cry | Harsh comments prove the kid was really bad
exercise: Coach says "sing from your diaphragm" and Lucas shouts. What did Coach mean? | Use steady, low breath support, not shouting | Sing as loudly as you possibly can | Push your stomach out hard and hold your breath while you sing | Sing only the lowest notes you have
exercise: What is the diaphragm? | A big breathing muscle under the lungs | A bone at the back of the throat that makes the voice | A part of the inner ear that hears notes | The sheet of music the choir sings from
exercise: Ivan says he is tone deaf "and it's genetic". What is true? | Real tone deafness is rare, and most people can improve | Most people who sing badly are truly tone deaf, and it runs in families | Tone deafness can never change with practice | Nobody in the world is tone deaf at all
exercise: Kevin says Grace is singing wrong, but she is doing the harmony. What is harmony? | Different notes sung together that sound good | Everybody in the choir singing exactly the same note together | Singing louder than the rest of the choir | A mistake that the teacher let slide
exercise: Ollie says Lin's Chinese folk song is "not real singing". What is true? | Every culture has its own singing styles | Only Western pop music counts as real singing | Folk songs are not allowed to be sung in schools | Chinese singing uses no real voice at all
exercise: Ella says the singer's mouth did not match the words. What might be happening? | Lip-syncing, which some singers do at big events | Every famous singer always sings live, at every single show | The TV is broken and shows the wrong singer | Her ears are wrong and need checking
exercise: Sam has a sore throat but wants to belt it out. What is wise? | Rest his voice, because forcing it can hurt it more | Sing louder to clear the throat | Push through it, because real singers never rest their voices | Shout instead of singing to save the voice
exercise: Morag cries after the concert, but her gran says she sang well. What is true? | Feelings after a show are mixed and prove nothing | Crying afterwards proves she sang badly, whatever her gran says | Only bad singers cry after shows | She should never sing again
exercise: What is vibrato? | A controlled wobble singers use on purpose | A nervous shake that only comes from stage fright | A type of microphone | A wrong note in a choir
exercise: What is amusia? | The medical name for real tone deafness | A kind of Italian opera sung in old theatres | A warm-up exercise | A famous choir in Kyiv
test: Your mate goes, "louder = better singing, obviously." Is he right? | No, good singing uses controlled breath, not shouting | Yes, the loudest singer always wins every competition | Yes, but only in choirs | No, singers should always whisper
test: What would happen if Sam belted out the concert with a really sore throat? | He could strain his voice and make it worse | His throat would heal faster | His voice would get permanently stronger from the hard work | Nothing at all would happen
test: Harry from Edinburgh says, "Boys cannae sing high, that's for lassies" ("cannae" = can't, "lassies" = girls). What is the best reply? | Plenty of boys sing high, like cathedral trebles | He's right, boys can't reach high notes, that's just how it is | Only lassies have vocal folds | High notes are banned for boys in Scotland
test: Gran tells Morag, "Dinnae greet, hen, ye sang braw!" What does she mean? | Don't cry, dear, you sang great | Don't sing again, it was bad | Don't eat, you need to warm up | Don't go, the show isn't over
test: A cartoon choir sings one note all together and calls any other note "a mistake". What's wrong with that? | Choirs often split into parts singing harmony | Nothing, choirs only ever sing one note, all together | All choirs sing without notes | Choirs never sing in tune
test: Ivan reckons he's tone deaf after one bad try. What would most likely happen if he practised for a term? | He would probably sing in tune much better | He would get worse and worse with every single lesson | Nothing could change at all | He would lose his voice for good
test: Kevin calls Grace a "header" (a wild, mad person) for singing a different note. Who is right? | Grace, because she is singing the harmony | Kevin, because every note must match | Neither, choirs have no parts | Kevin, because headers can't sing
test: Your sister says the talent show proves the judge is the nastiest person alive. What should she remember? | The show is cut and edited for drama | TV never edits anything | Judges always mean every word | Nastiness is in every judge's contract
test: Ella says, "She mimed, so she's a fake, end of." What is the fairest view? | Some call lip-syncing cheating, others say it protects the show | Every singer always lip-syncs, so nobody ever sings live on stage | Lip-syncing is against the law | Nobody has ever lip-synced in public
test: Sam in Cardiff says singing loud is "lush" (lovely) even with a sore throat. What should he do instead? | Rest his voice, drink water and avoid shouting | Sing even louder to get the concert over with quickly | Shout to warm the throat | Drink fizzy pop and carry on
test: Lin sings a folk song and Ollie laughs. What is the best way to listen to a new style? | With curiosity, because it is only new to you | With mockery, because it sounds strange to you | Not at all, because a song like that is not real music | Only if it sounds like the pop songs you already know
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the instruction to sing "from the diaphragm". | The employment of steady and controlled breath support | The production of the maximum possible volume | The restriction of the voice to its lowest register | The suspension of respiration during performance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the prevalence of true tone deafness. | It is reported to be rare, and most persons may improve with practice | It affects the majority of persons who sing poorly and is inherited in families | It is incapable of any amelioration by practice | It is unknown to medical science
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "harmony" as employed in choral singing? | The simultaneous singing of different notes that sound agreeably together | The singing of an identical note by every member | The singing of one member at a greater volume than the rest of the choir | An error tolerated by the director of the choir
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct of a singer suffering from a sore throat prior to a performance. | The resting of the voice and the avoidance of forceful singing | The singing at increased volume so as to clear the throat | The substitution of shouting for singing | The disregarding of the condition entirely
